@namespace IzzyBlazor
@typeparam TItem
@attribute [CascadingTypeParameter(nameof(TItem))]
@inherits GridViewBase<TItem>
@attribute [ModeSupport(NoScript = ModeLevel.Partial, NoScriptNotes = "With JavaScript off, there's no select-all checkbox and Shift+click doesn't add to the sort (the column menu's Add to sort does); filters apply when you press Apply; with columns hidden to fit, a row being edited shows their values in its detail, not fields.")]

@*
    A data grid: a real table, on the same columns, data, paging, filters and selection as Table,
    with sort, filter and page in the address, so it works on static pages. It adds what a grid
    needs: Shift+click on a header, or a column menu's Add to sort, sorts by several columns
    (?sort=customer,-total), and column widths are set by the server, so nothing moves when the
    script arrives.

    A column with a `Width` keeps it (a fixed length, not a percent). Columns without one share the rest of the space, down to their
    `MinWidth` (by default 8rem, or wider to fit its header), and then the grid scrolls sideways. If every column has a `Width`, the
    grid is as wide as their sum.

    `Frozen` keeps a column in view at the grid's start or end, with no script, and is drawn at that
    edge wherever it's declared. It needs a `Width`. On a phone they scroll like the rest, and so they
    do if the script finds they'd leave too little room for the rows. RowHref can't be used with it.

    `SmallScreen="DataGridSmallScreen.HideColumns"` hides columns that don't fit instead of scrolling,
    lowest `Priority` first, and a button at each row's start shows their values under it. The first
    column and frozen ones stay. It goes by the grid's own width, worked out on the server, so it's right
    at the first paint with no script. The grid takes the whole width it's given, and needs an ItemKey.

    <DataGrid Items="orders" ItemKey="o => o.Id" PageSize="20" Caption="Orders">
        <Columns>
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Id"
                            Title="Order"
                            Width="8.Rem"
                            Frozen="ColumnFrozen.Start" />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Customer" Sortable Filterable MinWidth="12.Rem" />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Total" Format="C" Sortable Width="10.Rem" />
        </Columns>
    </DataGrid>

    `Groupable` lets people group by columns from a header's menu, and `InitialGroup` or `Group` starts
    grouped. The grouping and what's open are in the address (`?group=status&open=Shipped`), so a group's
    row is a link and this works with no script. A page counts top-level groups, a group is never split,
    and whatever `Paging` or `Virtualize` say, a grouped grid draws numbered pages. `Aggregate` on a column
    works out a value over every row the filters match, for the footer and each group's row. With an
    ItemsProvider, either one needs a GroupsProvider: rows still come from your provider, with the group's
    values in the filter.

    `LayoutKey` remembers how one person has arranged the columns (widths, order, hidden, frozen) in a
    cookie, so the server draws it on the first paint with no flash. It's per person and per page; a
    shared link still shows the same rows, since sort, filter and page are in the address. To keep
    layouts in a database instead, bind Layout.

    `ResizableColumns`, `ReorderableColumns` and `ColumnPicker` let people rearrange the columns, with
    script, by pointer, keys, a menu in each header and a picker above the grid. On a static page they
    need somewhere to keep the result: a LayoutKey, or Layout with a LayoutSaveUrl.

    A toolbar above the grid holds `ToolbarContent` (Toolbar's items), the search box with `Searchable`,
    export and the Columns button, which show their icons alone unless ToolbarLabels is set. Write
    DataGridSearch, DataGridExport or DataGridColumnPicker in ToolbarContent to place one among your items, or
    outside the grid (a Tile's HeaderEnd, or anywhere with For set to the grid's id) to move it there. While rows are selected, the bar of SelectionActions takes its place. `Searchable`
    searches the text columns; the words go in the address (?search=), and with script apply as
    you type.

    `Virtualize` draws only the rows in view, for tens of thousands of them, on an interactive page: give
    it a MaxHeight, and keep rows to one line of RowHeight. An ItemsProvider gets each window as a `Skip`
    and `Take`, and returns the `Total`. On a static page it loads on scroll instead, by PageSize (50 if
    not set), up to 2,000 rows. If the focused row scrolls away, focus waits on the scroll box and goes back when it
    returns, or on the next key, which scrolls to it. Browser find, and Shift ranges of selection,
    reach the rows drawn, unless `SelectAllMode` is `All`.

    `ExportExcelHref`, and `ExportCsvHref` beside it if CSV is wanted too, add plain links to endpoints the
    app maps with `DataExport`. A `DataGridExportFormat` among the columns adds another format,
    such as PDF. With more than one, a button with a chevron opens a menu of them. They
    carry the filter, sort, search and visible columns, in every mode and with no script. The
    endpoint's fields decide what's written, and head the file. A download matches the screen when
    they have the grid's keys: a column whose key they don't have is left out, a filter on it matches
    nothing, and a sort on it is ignored. Search covers their searchable fields, which may not be the
    grid's text columns. Text doesn't carry `FilterCase`, so a grid that sets it needs the same `Case` on
    those fields. `ExportHref` gives the same address for a button of your own, and a column with
    `Exportable="false"` stays out of the file.

    `ActionColumn` holds a row's actions in a last column: EditAction, DeleteAction and your own
    RowActions, each a button, or with Overflow="RowActionOverflow.Always" in a ⋮ menu, which works
    with no script. In a grid that hides columns, buttons move into the menu once one is hidden, unless
    they say `Never`. A grid with editing has one by itself. With script a right-click on the row, a long
    press, Shift+F10 or the menu key opens the row's menu. It acts on the row it opened on, and doesn't
    change the selection; actions on the selection go in the bar. A right-click on a link keeps the
    browser's menu, with its "open in new tab": that includes the whole of a row with RowHref, whose ⋮
    button and Shift+F10 still open the menu.

    `EditMode` with `OnSave` edits a row, with no script needed. Each column gets a field chosen by its type
    (a text area for text with [DataType(DataType.MultilineText)]) if it can be edited, shown or not:
    set Editable="false" on what can't be, such as the key, which a save can't change. Visible="false"
    starts a column hidden, for a field the table doesn't show, or for people to show from the picker. Each row has an Edit link, which puts
    its key in the address (?edit=42), and a save is a form post. Only editable columns take a value
    from it, so a forged field changes nothing. OnSave gets a copy of the row, and either finishes,
    which closes the editor and loads the rows again, or adds errors, which stay on the fields. A grid
    with editing can't be inside another form, as forms can't nest. It needs an ItemKey, and on a
    static page, a row type Blazor can fill from a form: public settable properties.

    <DataGrid Items="orders"
              ItemKey="o => o.Id.ToString()"
              EditMode="DataGridEditMode.Dialog"
              OnSave="SaveAsync">
        <Columns>
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Id" Editable="false" />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Customer" />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Notes" Visible="false" />
        </Columns>
    </DataGrid>

    `DataGridEditMode.Row` edits where the row stands, with Save and Cancel in place of its actions, also
    with no script. Only the columns shown have a field, so a hidden one keeps its value. A new row
    starts at the top of the page. Sorting, filtering or changing page ends the edit, and what's typed
    goes with it. It can't be used with Virtualize or loading on scroll, or with an EditDialogForm.

    `DataGridEditMode.Cell` edits one cell at a time on an interactive page, and OnSave runs for each one
    that changed. A click, Enter, F2 or typing starts it; Tab, Enter or a click elsewhere saves it, and an
    invalid cell stays open. A new row is edited as with `Row`, and so is every row on a static page. Besides
    the limits of `Row`, it can't be used with `RowHref`.

    `ReorderableRows` puts a handle at each row's start: drag it, or use its menu, to put the rows in
    their own order. It needs an ItemKey and every row in Items, and the handles work only while the
    grid isn't sorted, filtered, searched or grouped, so leave those off: the order is the data. On interactive pages bind `@bind-Items`; on static
    pages give it an `OrderName` inside a form and pass the posted ListOrder back as `Order`.

    Accessibility:
    - `Caption` names the grid for screen readers; it isn't shown.
    - With script it's an ARIA grid, one Tab stop, with the keys below. A cell with one link, button or
      checkbox focuses that directly. With no script, it's a plain table and Tab goes through its
      controls.
    - Resizing and moving a column need no dragging: a header's keys and menu do both, and a new width
      is read out.
    - Sortable headers are links, and the first sorted column has `aria-sort`. With several sorted,
      each says its place and direction ("Sort 2, descending").
    - Row checkboxes and filter buttons are named as in a Table.
    - A column in a `ColumnBand` is read with the band's name before its own.
    - With `ReorderableRows`, each row's handle is named "Move" and the row's label, and its menu
      moves the row without dragging. Every move is announced: "Moved Ada to position 3 of 5".
    - The toolbar is one Tab stop with script, with arrow keys between its controls. The tools that
      show only an icon are named for what they do, and have that as a hover tooltip.

    Keyboard:
    In the rows
    - Tab | Move into the grid, and on past it
    - Up, Down | Move to the row above or below
    - Left, Right | Move to the cell before or after
    - Home, End | Move to the first or last cell of the row
    - Ctrl+Home, Ctrl+End | Move to the first cell of the first row, or the last cell of the last
    - Page Up, Page Down | Move by the rows in view; from the edge of a page, to the next page
    - Enter, F2 | Go into a cell with several controls or a field
    - F2 | Edit the row, from a cell of text, in a grid that edits rows
    - Enter | On a row that's a link, follow it, from a cell of text
    - Alt+Down | Open the cell's menu
    - Alt+Up, Alt+Down, Alt+Home, Alt+End | On a row's handle, move the row
    - Alt+Left, Alt+Right | On a row's handle, move the row before or after (reversed on
      right-to-left pages)
    - Shift+F10, Menu | Open the row's menu (on a link, only Shift+F10)
    Inside a cell
    - Tab | Move among its controls, and back out past either end
    - Up, Down, Left, Right | On a button or link, move on to another cell; a field keeps them
    - Escape, F2 | Go back to the cell
    Groups and details
    - Right, Left | On a group's row, open or close it
    - Enter | On a group's row, or a row's detail button, open or close it
    - Down | From a row, move to its open detail, one stop whatever it holds
    - Enter, F2 | Go into the detail
    - Escape | Go back to the detail from inside it, a grid in it too
    - Tab | Inside the detail, move among its controls; from a grid in it, on past this grid
    In a row being edited
    - Tab, Shift+Tab | Move through its fields, then Save and Cancel
    - Enter | In a field, save
    - Escape | Cancel, once any open list or picker has closed
    With DataGridEditMode.Cell, on an interactive page
    - Enter, F2, typing | Edit the cell, from a cell that can be edited
    - Tab, Shift+Tab | Save it, and edit the next or the one before
    - Enter | Save it, and go back to the cell
    - Escape | Cancel, and go back to the cell
    Selecting rows
    - Space | Select or clear the row, from a cell of text
    - Shift+Space | Select or clear the row, from anywhere in it but a field
    - Shift+Up, Shift+Down | Extend the selection, or shrink it going back
    - Ctrl+A | Select what's on the page, or the rows drawn when they're virtual; every match with SelectAllMode.All
    On a header
    - Enter | Sort by the column, if it sorts
    - Shift+Enter | Add the column to the sort; again, reverse it, then take it out
    - Alt+Down | Open its menu, or its filter when there's no menu
    - F2 | Go in to its controls, the sort link first
    - Shift+F10, Menu | Open its menu
    - Shift+Left, Shift+Right | Resize the column, where it can be
    - Alt+Left, Alt+Right | Move the column, where it can be
    - Page Down | Go to the first row
    In the bar of what's grouped
    - Alt+Left, Alt+Right | On a chip's remove control, move that level before or after (reversed
      on right-to-left pages)
    - Shift+F10, Menu key | On a chip's remove control, open its menu (Move left, Move right),
      as a click on the chip does
    In the footer
    - Tab | Move through the page size button and the pager's links
    - Enter | Follow the link
    - Page Up | Go to the last row
    In the filter row
    - Up, Down | Move to the header above or the row below
    - Left, Right | Move to the filter before or after
    - Enter, F2 | Go into the filter's field
    - Alt+Down | Open the column's filter panel
    Inside a filter cell
    - Tab, Shift+Tab | Move between the field and the filter button, and back out past either end
    - Enter | In the field, filter now without waiting
    - Alt+Down | In the field, open the column's filter panel
    - Escape, F2 | Go back to the cell; the filter stays
    In a filter panel
    - Tab, Shift+Tab | Move among the operator, the values and the list
    - Enter | Filter now and close, from a field or the list's search box
    - Escape | Close and go back to the filter button; the filter stays
    In a filter's list
    - Down | Move from the search box to Select all, then on through the values
    - Up | Move back through the values to Select all, then the search box
    - Home, End | Move to the first or last value
    - Space | Tick or untick the value
    In the column chooser
    - Alt+Up, Alt+Down, Alt+Home, Alt+End | On a row that moves, move the column
    While dragging
    - Escape | Cancel dragging a column, its edge, a row, a group or a chooser row
    See also: Table.
*@

@Grid
